"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[49],{3049:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2018-11-23T09:49:00","modified":"2018-11-23T09:49:00","id":1040,"title":"Typescript and Babel 7 with/without Webpack","article":"<p>So now babel 7 has been released and it has typescript support, epic win! However that&#39;s not the end of the story...</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"tldr\\">TL;DR</h2>\\n<p>Babel 7 now supports compiling typescript to javascript, but doesnt actually type check your code to see if its types are valid. </p>\\n<p>There are a couple of ways to type checking, you can either run webpack (or babel directly) at the same time (or in sequence) with the Typecript compiler (<code>tsc</code>), or use an existing webpack plugin <a href=\\"https://github.com/Realytics/fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin\\">fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin</a>. Although it was designed to work with <code>ts-loader</code>, <code>ts-loader</code> is not required, so you just need to add it to your webpack build.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"whats-the-problem\\">Whats the problem?</h2>\\n<p>Adding typescript support to Babel 7 is really easy, it is just install then add a preset called <code>@babel/preset-typescript</code>. But what does this do? Well it means when running babel against a .ts/.tsx file it will compile it happily the same way as any other javascript file.</p>\\n<p>But it does <strong>NOT</strong> type check your code and isnt that <strong>the whole point of using Typescript?</strong> Saying that this is definitely a step up.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"how-does-webpack-fit\\">How does Webpack fit?</h2>\\n<p>When using webpack it has been possible to use babel to compile files but using Typescript to type check for a while, I have been using it on my website for about 2 years. You just had to use a specific loader to handle ts/tsx files and had two choices: <a href=\\"https://github.com/TypeStrong/ts-loader\\">ts-loader</a> and <a href=\\"https://github.com/s-panferov/awesome-typescript-loader\\">awesome-ts-loader</a>. Each scratches the itch in a different way, <em>see their Readme&#39;s for differences but for most small to medium projects either is fine.</em></p>\\n<p>That setup still works fine, however there were some caveats, namely it would partly convert the ts/tsx code to javascript to make it more compatible as well as type checking it. Then babel would then be able to convert it. </p>\\n<p>Despite using tsconfig.json settings to minimise the amount it converted, it could create some unexpected inconsistancies. E.g. when importing from node_modules with webpack where the mainFields property was set to module first, typescript would ignore that.</p>\\n<p>So using babel to handle more of the conversion process helps eliminate those issues, after all once we know the types are valid, we just want them stripped.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"solutions\\">Solutions?</h2>\\n<p>Well there are two solutions I know of currently, one is running the <code>tsc</code> compiler separately from babel or webpack, getting feedback and error codes from that regardless of whether it compiles via babel. The other solution is to use an existing package <a href=\\"https://github.com/Realytics/fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin\\">fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin</a> that will allow us to plug directly into webpacks build process (obviously thats not an option for babel on its own just webpack).</p>\\n<p>Regardless of the way you want to do it, you will need to create a <code>tsconfig.json</code> and install <code>@babel/preset-typescript</code> with babel.</p>\\n<p>Create a tsconfig.json, the most important setting being &#39;noEmit&#39; as we dont want it creating anything, just checking the types:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\">{\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"compilerOptions\\"</span>: {\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Target latest version of ECMAScript.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"target\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"esnext\\"</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Search under node_modules for non-relative imports.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"moduleResolution\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"node\\"</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Process &amp; infer types from .js files.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"allowJs\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Don\'t emit; allow Babel to transform files.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"noEmit\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Enable strictest settings like strictNullChecks &amp; noImplicitAny.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"strict\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Disallow features that require cross-file information for emit.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"isolatedModules\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Import non-ES modules as default imports.</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"esModuleInterop\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>\\n  },\\n  <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"include\\"</span>: [\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"src\\"</span>\\n  ]\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>Install <code>@babel/preset-typescript</code> along with babel packages if you havent already:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npm i --save-dev @babel/core @babel/cli @babel/preset-env @babel/preset-typescript typescript</code></pre>\\n<p>Create <code>.babelrc</code>:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\">{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"presets\\"</span>: [\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"@babel/env\\"</span>,\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"@babel/preset-typescript\\"</span>\\n    ]\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h3 id=\\"running-webpackbabel-and-type-checking-separately\\">Running webpack/Babel and type checking separately</h3>\\n<p>The above is all the configuration you need to do, then to type check and build you can run the following command in the terminal:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npx tsc &amp;&amp; npx webpack-cli</code></pre>\\n<p>When developing though you would normally want to run a watch on files or maybe a dev server. The command above will not work because the first command would never end. So in that scenario you would need to open two terminals and run two commands: <em>(the watch parameter is optional)</em></p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npx tsc --watch</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npx webpack-cli --watch \\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\"># or if running babel </span>\\nnpx babel ./src --out-dir dist --extensions <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\".ts,.tsx\\"</span> --watch</code></pre>\\n<h3 id=\\"running-webpack-and-type-checking-together\\">Running Webpack and type checking together</h3>\\n<p>Although the above solution works, it isnt ideal as it can get tricky with watches and things like the webpack-dev-server.</p>\\n<p>Fortunately an existing plugin <a href=\\"https://github.com/Realytics/fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin\\">fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin</a> was created for <code>ts-loader</code>. It was originally created because type checking on lots of files can be slower than simply compiling them, so this plugin runs the type checking in another process to the compiling in webpack. It has no dependency on <code>ts-loader</code> either so we can use it in our Babel 7 setup without a dependency.</p>\\n<p>First install <code>fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin</code>, along with webpack if not already installed:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npm i --save-dev webpack webpack-cli fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin</code></pre>\\n<p>And it to your existing webpack.config.js setup E.g.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> ForkTsCheckerwebpackPlugin = <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin\'</span>);\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">module</span>.exports = <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> (<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">env, argv</span>) </span>{\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">devtool</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'cheap-source-map\'</span>,\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">entry</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'./src/index.ts\'</span>,\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">module</span>: {\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">rules</span>: [\\n            {\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">test</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-regexp\\">/\\\\.jsx?|\\\\.tsx?$/</span>,\\n                exclude: [<span class=\\"hljs-regexp\\">/node_modules/</span>],\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">use</span>: { <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">loader</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'babel-loader\'</span> }\\n            }, \\n            ]\\n        },\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> ForkTsCheckerwebpackPlugin()\\n        ]\\n    };\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>Finally run webpack as normal:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npx webpack-cli --watch</code></pre>\\n<p>If either there is a syntax or type error then the webpack cli will return an error code and display the error to stdout (the console).</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"summary\\">Summary</h2>\\n<p>Its relatively simply to setup Babel 7, Typescript and webpack together so that it not only does syntax checking, but also type checking as well. There are just a couple of added considerations, but the benefit of using Typescript is definitely worth it.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"references\\">References</h2>\\n<ul>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://babeljs.io/blog/2018/08/27/7.0.0#typescript-support-babel-preset-typescript\\">https://babeljs.io/blog/2018/08/27/7.0.0#typescript-support-babel-preset-typescript</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/typescript/2018/08/27/typescript-and-babel-7/\\">https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/typescript/2018/08/27/typescript-and-babel-7/</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://github.com/Realytics/fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin\\">https://github.com/Realytics/fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["webpack","typescript","babel"]}')}}]);